== Interrupts
Authors and vendors use different terms to refer to the same concept, a way of
stopping the current execution of the program due to an exceptional situation.
In this thesis, we'll use _interrupt_.

=== Interrupt Taxonomy
Patterson and Hennery @Patterson2021Exceptions use the term _exception_ to refer
to #quote([[a]n unescheduled event that disrupts program execution; used to
  detect undefined instructions]), defining _interrupt_ as #quote([[a]n
  exception that comes from outside the processor]). Bulić @Bulić2024Interrupts,
on the other hand, differentiates between _interrupts_ and _traps_. He defines
interrupts as #quote([signals generated by hardware peripherals or devices to
  signal the processor of an event that requires attention]), and distinguishes
between two types: _software_ interrupts, which #quote([function similarly to
  subroutine calls]) and #quote(
  [are usually used for requesting operating system services],
), and _external_ interrupts, which are triggered by #quote([[a]n external
  device [...] activating the interrupt request pin on the CPU]). He further
divides the latter into _maskable_, which #quote([can be enabled or disabled by
  setting a bit in the CPU's control register or executing a special
  instruction]), and _non-maskable_, which #quote([have a higher priority than
  maskable interrupts]). On the other hand, he defines _traps_ as #quote(
  [internally generated signals that indicate exceptional conditions or errors
    encountered during the execution of program executions],
) that #quote([require special handling by the processor or operating system to
  resolve and recover from the error]).

Undergraduate courses on computer architecture and organization, such as the
ones from the Northern Illinois University @Winans2021Interrupts and Universidad
Carlos III de Madrid @Carballeira2025Interrupts classify _interrupts_ as either
_syncronous_ or _asyncronous_. _Syncronous_ interrupts are generated when an
instruction fails as a result of an error, or by special system call
instructions, and abort the execution of the current instruction; _asyncronous_
interrupts (also called _interrupt requests_) are generated by either a device,
or a timer, and wait for the instruction to finish. This distinction is mainly
relevant in pipelined architectures, where one instruction is performed during
several clock cycles, and an error during the execution of an instruction
compromises the pipeline and requires inmmediate attention.


=== Interrupt Detection
CPUs that support interrupts have an interupt request (_IRQ_) pin, sometimes
mapped to a register, that signals that an interrupt has occurred.


=== Interrupt Handling
In order to treat with the interrupt, the state of the CPU is stored, and the
interrupt handler is executed.

